AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate strong performance on isolated code generation tasks, yet interactive website development introduces challenges that existing approaches fail to address reliably. As projects evolve across multiple iterations combining AI-generated code with manual developer edits, current systems, whether relying on conversational memory or traditional retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), treat retrieved context as advisory suggestions rather than authoritative project state. This leads to overwritten user edits, hallucinated dependencies, and unpredictable behavior across development iterations. This paper introduces State-RAG, a dual-layer retrieval architecture that explicitly separates reusable design knowledge (Global RAG) from authoritative project state (State-RAG). The State-RAG layer stores validated, versioned software artifacts representing the definitive state of each project component, page, and configuration file. An orchestration pipeline coordinates retrieval, prompt construction, LLM generation, multi-stage validation, and artifact commitment while enforcing strict authority boundaries between user modifications and AI-generated code. The LLM is treated as a stateless transformation engine operating only over explicitly provided inputs, with no implicit memory of project history. A pilot evaluation across eight structured development scenarios and a ten-step interaction chain demonstrates that State-RAG achieves 88% scope adherence (versus 12% for Conv-History and 50% for Single-RAG), 100% authority probe blocking, zero hallucinations in both isolated cases and long interaction chains, and an average token cost of 662 tokens per request - an 82% reduction compared to Conv-History (3,611 tokens) and Single-RAG (3,745 tokens).
