AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated exceptional performance in source code generation across multiple programming languages. However, LLM-generated code often suffers from inconsistent style, structural redundancy, and potential security vulnerabilities. Therefore, source attribution for generated code is crucial for identifying the model origin, evaluating quality, and enhancing reliability. Due to strict syntactic constraints, code generated by different models for the same prompt exhibits much higher similarity than natural language text, causing existing text source attribution methods to degrade when applied to code attribution. To address this and analyze stylistic differences, we propose the Code Generation Source Detection (CGSD) framework. It first employs Supervised Contrastive Learning to obtain distinguishable code embeddings and trains a classifier to determine the code’s origin. CGSD then combines a similarity analysis module with the code style analyzer to deeply analyze coding styles of different models. Experiments on a self-constructed, multi-class dataset containing code from various mainstream LLMs show that CGSD achieves 93.25% accuracy, significantly outperforming existing baselines. This confirms that CGSD can both effectively capture model-specific generation patterns and reveal systematic stylistic differences across models.
ER  - 

