AB  - Game analytics depends on high-volume game play telemetry, but Large Language Models (LLMs) cannot directly operate on full logs due to token limits, schema variability, and compute cost. Ad hoc prompt-based use of LLMs is brittle, difficult to reproduce, and prone to hallucination. We present GameLogLLM, a context-efficient framework for automated analysis of competitive gameplay data with LLMs. The framework has four stages: (i) extraction of structural and experimental context from raw gameplay logs and documentation; (ii) controlled prompt construction that feeds this context to the model under explicit constraints; (iii) rule-guided code generation that produces executable Python to compute validated tables and latency-performance visualizations; and (iv) evidence-linked insight generation, where a second LLM produces human-readable findings grounded in those results. In evaluation on inducedlatency gameplay data, GameLogLLM reproduces expert quantitative analysis and produces domain-relevant narrative insights using locally hosted 7B-14B parameter LLMs, without external APIs, supporting reproducible and privacy-preserving analytics.
ER  - 

