AB  - Repeated calls to a large language model (LLM) can produce programs that pass the same tests but differ in structure. We quantify this variation with five standardized Python abstract syntax tree (AST) descriptors. Our model-agnostic framework measures repeat consistency with the Structural Stability Index (SSI), prompt-centroid distances with the Prompt Sensitivity Index (PSSI), and total dispersion with the Structural Diversity Score (SDS). The primary experiment used 164 HumanEval tasks, three LLM conditions, five instruction-oriented prompts, and five repetitions. Of 12,300 generations, 12,287 were AST-parsable and 11,766 passed the original tests. Claude Sonnet 4.6 led both pass rate and SSI under the evaluated provider settings. GPT-5 mini combined a high pass rate with the lowest SSI and highest PSSI and SDS. SSI correlated with reduced-AST edit stability (ρ = 0.969, N = 2,459). HumanEval+ lowered the EvalPlus base pass rate from 95.13% to 90.33%. A second experiment produced 12,300 generations using neutral paraphrases, with mean PSSI 57.1% lower on a common scale. Collection dates and hosted model identifiers also differed, preventing attribution to prompt family alone.Within-run prompt-label permutations found separation beyond random partitioning, with finite-repeat variation still present. Correlations with ordered-subtree PSSI/SDS were ρ = 0.586/0.700 for the primary run and 0.851/0.897 for paraphrases. These measures describe structural variation left unspecified by test outcomes; semantic correctness and software quality require separate evaluation.
ER  - 

