AB  - The rapid adoption of large language models (LLMs), such as ChatGPT, in education has intensified the need to differentiate artificial intelligence (AI)-generated from human-authored student work. This study proposes an intelligence augmentation (IA) framework that evaluates algorithmic performance while incorporating a human-in-the-loop mechanism for practical classroom deployment. Leveraging a comprehensive multitask dataset that spans article generation, text paraphrasing, and code generation, we conduct a systematic comparison between traditional machine learning and deep learning models. Beyond benchmark evaluations, the proposed approach is further validated using authentic classroom data, achieving up to 85% detection accuracy—significantly surpassing conventional algorithms and state-of-the-art zero-shot baselines. The results present the first multitask evaluation of generated-text detection within an IA framework, demonstrating the broad adaptability of both classical machine learning and deep learning algorithms across educational scenarios. These findings offer empirical evidence and actionable insights for developing trustworthy IA systems that strengthen academic integrity in AI-assisted learning environments.
ER  - 

