AB  - Cognitive complexity, a structural proxy for comprehension effort, is widely used to compare the code produced by large language models (LLMs), but little is known about how well the metric discriminates on the short, self-contained tasks that dominate code generation benchmarks. This study analyzes 3,000 Python solutions generated by Claude Sonnet 4.6, Gemini 2.5 Pro, and GPT-5.4 for the 500 tasks of the MBPP test split under two prompt strategies, of which 1,914 passed the benchmark’s own test cases and were measured with SonarScanner. The models differ significantly in cognitive complexity, with Claude producing the least complex and GPT the most complex code. Cognitive complexity correlates almost perfectly with cyclomatic complexity, strongly with lines of code, and only weakly with code smells. The differences rest on a minority of tasks. Across the 534 blocks in which all three models were correct, 46.8% are identical for all models, 37.5% are zero throughout, and every median pairwise difference is zero. A stratification by task difficulty derived from the MBPP reference solutions shows that this floor effect is concentrated in easy tasks: the share of identical blocks falls from 62.6% to 24.2% between the easiest and the hardest tercile. Model differences do not grow monotonically with difficulty. We conclude that cognitive complexity is a defensible measure of structural differences where benchmark tasks are structurally demanding, and that studies reporting significance without tie rates risk presenting an effect carried by a small fraction of their data.
ER  - 

