AB  - With the widespread application of large language models (LLMs) in the field of code generation, the security of their generated code has become a critical issue in software engineering and security research. This study systematically analyzes security vulnerability patterns in artificial intelligence (AI)-generated code, elaborates on methods and tools for vulnerability assessment, and summarizes common vulnerability types. A comparative analysis of human-written and AI-generated code samples reveals that AI-generated code exhibits a high security defect rate and is highly dependent on the precision of prompts and the completeness of context. The study further highlights that the security of AI-generated code can be significantly enhanced by optimizing input validation mechanisms, refining boundary condition processing, and improving the model’s ability to comprehend context, thereby providing a reliable assurance for its widespread deployment in real-world production environments.
