AB  - Large Language Models (LLM) are increasingly used to automate code generation tasks, including RESTful API development. However, empirical evidence on how different artefact representations influence the quality and reliability of LLM-generated REST API code remains limited. This paper evaluates two database-related representations, JSON and Data Definition Language (DDL), on LLM for RESTful API code generation. Three experimental settings with increasing levels of complexity are established. The generated code is evaluated through static and behavioural analyses that focus on code completeness, complexity, and runtime functional correctness. The results show that input artefact representation affects code generation output. DDL achieves higher completeness (86.2–97.8%) and better handles more complex relational schemas, while JSON produces simpler, more readable code, but completeness degrades (to 46%) under complex scenarios. Therefore, many-to-many database relationships and complex structures remain a challenge for LLM-based RESTful API code generation. These results highlight trade-offs among completeness, structural complexity, and runtime reliability and provide empirical insights for designing more effective input representations and prompting strategies for automated RESTful API development.
ER  - 

