AB  - This paper investigates the feasibility of using large language models to assist in the early stages of digital twin development by generating Unity simulation components from structured descriptions. A locally hosted pipeline was implemented to support this workflow, combining structured prompting, model-to-engine communication, code extraction, and automated compilation feedback. Two models, Qwen 3 Coder 30B and Code Llama Instruct 13B, were evaluated using identical behavioral specifications to assess differences in generation time, compilation success, and functional correctness. Both models produced valid Unity scripts, but exhibited distinct reasoning patterns: Qwen demonstrated stronger contextual interpolation, while Code Llama performed more reliably under precise, narrowly scoped instructions. The experiments also revealed practical limits related to prompt length and model stability. The results suggest that language models can contribute meaningfully to early-stage digital twin prototyping, while highlighting the need for refined prompting strategies or domain-specific finetuning to improve consistency and scalability.
