AB  - As Large Language Models (LLMs) increasingly assist software development, their ability to match the rigor of automated theorem provers for Map-Reduce code generation remains unclear. Existing evaluations treat Python-to-Spark translation as a black box, assessing models only by binary pass or fail outcomes for generated code. This obscures whether models truly understand the logical deductions required for translating nontrivial Python programs into semantically equivalent Apache Spark code. To bridge this gap, we build on our prior work-a Python preprocessor that uses formal verification to guarantee semantic equivalence of generated Spark code-and introduce SCoT-Lift, a framework that lifts the preprocessor’s Dafny verification traces into high-level, human-readable Synthesis Chain-of-Thought (SCoT) steps. By exposing the formal reasoning underlying each translation as an explicit SCoT, SCoT-Lift provides concrete ground truth for finegrained evaluation. Leveraging SCoT-Lift, we introduce SCoT-Bench, a comprehensive benchmark of 1,652 SCoT completion tasks for rigorously evaluating LLMs on the Python-to-Spark translation process. SCoT-Bench measures performance along three orthogonal dimensions: robustness to varying degrees of missing translation steps, competence across different synthesis construct types, and sensitivity to the position of missing reasoning steps. Evaluation of ten state-of-the-art LLMs reveals severe fragility, indicating that current LLMs fall well short of the reasoning capabilities exhibited by automated theorem provers, and lag significantly behind the correctness guarantees of our formal Python preprocessor.
ER  - 

