AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) have become core tools for industrial code generation, but DocStrings—the primary prompt carriers—often suffer from severe information redundancy, directly increasing model inference computational costs and third-party API expenses. Existing general-purpose prompt compression methods exhibit significant adaptability flaws in code generation scenarios: code quality drops sharply when compression rates exceed 10%, and fixed compression rates cannot adapt to varying DocString information densities across different tasks. To address these issues, this paper proposes SemCompress, a semantic-aware adaptive DocString compression method, which achieves a dynamic balance between compression efficiency and generation quality through three core modules: code-semantic-anchored structured parsing, dual-weighted token importance quantification, and dynamic-threshold-constrained iterative compression. Experiments on three mainstream code generation benchmarks—HumanEval, CodeHarmony, and Big-CodeBench—show that SemCompress achieves stable compression rates of 25% to 35%, outperforms state-of-the-art baselines by an average of 3.7 percentage points in Pass@1, reduces model inference floating-point operations (FLOPs) by an average of 18.7%, and shortens end-to-end inference latency by up to 21.4%.
