AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly being used for software sustaining engineering tasks such as defect repair, refactoring, migration, and configuration updates. Concerns about reliability, explainability, governance, and the introduction of defects persist despite good results in some scenarios. Existing approaches rely on prompt engineering or on orchestrating a set of autonomous agents to control the workflow and enforce policy. These are delegated to probabilistic LLMs. This paper argues that reliable AI-assisted software sustaining engineering is primarily a function of executable control, rather than model scale or prompt sophistication. We present a control-centric interpretation of ATeam, a governanceaware framework that structures software sustainment as a fixed sequence of execution phases, each enforcing a distinct class of governance controls across six dimensions: semantic, architectural, autonomy, temporal, organizational, and regulatory. We evaluate the given framework on 24 IEEE 14764-aligned sustaining engineering tasks across multiple LLM sizes (2B to 20B parameters). We compare governed execution using ATeam with several prompt-based approaches, including an unconstrained prompt and a structured reasoning prompt. The results show that ATeam phase-based control yields weighted correctness scores of $\mathbf{6 6 - 8 3 \%}$ versus $\mathbf{2 1 - 6 3 \%}$ for prompt-based approaches, with significantly lower variance and strong auditability. The promptbased approaches exhibit significant performance variability and can suffer catastrophic failure and instability. Smaller governed models outperform larger ungoverned baselines across all conditions, demonstrating that execution structure - not model intelligence - is the primary determinant of reliability.
ER  - 

