AB  - Large language Models (LLMs) have been widely applied in robotics such as executable code generation. The transferability and convenience offered by in-context learning (ICL)-based embodied artificial intelligence (EAI) significantly enhance efficiency but also introduce security risks, particularly backdoor attacks. Although several security mechanisms have been proposed, such as incorporating additional prompts for risk analysis or requiring LLMs to perform multi-round reasoning on the generated code, these approaches substantially increase computational overhead and costs while still exhibiting high false positive rates. In this paper, we propose a plug-and-play framework named Transcriber, which functions as a trigger filter to defend against backdoor attacks in ICL-based EAI. Transcriber effectively reduces both the false positive rate and trigger survival rate across various types of triggers compared with a wide adopted lightweight detection methods. Furthermore, we introduce a dual trigger filter mechanism to mitigate false positives and enhance overall filtering performance. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our approach preserves task executability for benign instructions, while effectively removing triggers from malicious ones, thereby defending against backdoor attacks in EAI.
